<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">TASR: Training-Free Adaptive Stopping for Iterative Retrieval</h1>
<span class="ltx_pubnotes ltx_pubnotes_meta"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_conference"><span class="ltx_note_name">Conference: </span>Workshop on AI Agent for Information Retrieval at KDD ’26; August 09–13, 2026; Jeju, Korea</span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_ccs"><span class="ltx_note_name">CCS: </span>Information systems Question answering</span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_ccs"><span class="ltx_note_name">CCS: </span>Information systems Retrieval models and ranking</span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_ccs"><span class="ltx_note_name">CCS: </span>Computing methodologies Natural language generation</span></span></span>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Adrian Kieback
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">James Silberrad Brown Center for AI</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">San Diego State University</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">, </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Uyiosa Philip Amadasun
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">James Silberrad Brown Center for AI</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">San Diego State University</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">, </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Aman Chadha
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id5" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">Stanford University</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id6" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">James Silberrad Brown Center for AI</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id7" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">Google DeepMind</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before"> and </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Aaron Elkins
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id8" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">James Silberrad Brown Center for AI</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id9" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">San Diego State University</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Iterative retrieval-augmented generation agents commonly overspend by continuing to retrieve after the model has converged on an answer, incurring calls that change neither the prediction nor the supporting evidence. Existing remedies learn a stopping policy from labeled trajectories, tying the decision to a trained component that requires retraining for each new model or task. We propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TASR</span> (Training-Free Adaptive Stopping Rule), a one-line predicate that fires when the model repeats its previous-round normalized answer <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">and</em> the isotonically calibrated logit margin exceeds <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.25</annotation></semantics></math>. No classifier or value head is learned; the threshold is fixed across all twenty-four (model, retriever, corpus) configurations we evaluate. On a <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-model <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dataset distractor grid, TASR retains <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="94.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>94.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">94.8\%</annotation></semantics></math> of fixed-<math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}5</annotation></semantics></math>’s macro F1 at <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="62.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>62.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">62.6\%</annotation></semantics></math> of its calls and exceeds fixed-<math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}3</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+3.42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3.42</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+3.42</annotation></semantics></math> F1. The pattern holds on nine open-domain BM25 cells (<math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="55.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>55.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">55.01</annotation></semantics></math> F1 at <math id="abstract1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.98" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.98</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.98</annotation></semantics></math> calls vs. <math id="abstract1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="54.33" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>54.33</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">54.33</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="abstract1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.00" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.00</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.00</annotation></semantics></math> for fixed-<math id="abstract1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}3</annotation></semantics></math>) and, with calibration locked from the distractor split, on nine dense-retrieval cells across two retriever families, with zero significant regressions in either extension. The rule was selected from an exhaustive enumeration of <math id="abstract1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="381" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>381</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">381</annotation></semantics></math> candidate stopping rules; no alternative Pareto-dominates it on any evaluated configuration. A signal-quality analysis shows that verbalized <math id="abstract1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="abstract1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> confidence collapses on RLHF-tuned models (<math id="abstract1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="96.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>96.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">96.5\%</annotation></semantics></math> of values equal <math id="abstract1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>, entropy <math id="abstract1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.182" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.182</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.182</annotation></semantics></math> nats), while the logit margin achieves <math id="abstract1.m21" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="44\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>44</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">44\times</annotation></semantics></math> better class-conditional separation, grounding the design in a measurable model pathology. TASR is an auditable, training-free Pareto baseline against which learned stopping controllers can be compared. Code is publicly available.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
        https://github.com/JSBAICenter/TASR</span></span></span></p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>retrieval-augmented generation, adaptive stopping, multi-hop question answering, training-free agents, calibration
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) Large Language Model (LLM) alternates between retrieving evidence and generating partial answers. Each retrieval requires a call to an external system (e.g., a vector store, BM25 index, or search API), followed by a call to the LLM. Both incur time, monetary, and energy costs, and inference is now the dominant contributor to large-scale LLM energy consumption <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luccioni et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Reducing the average number of calls per query therefore lowers all three costs simultaneously.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A common failure mode of iterative retrieval is overspending: the agent continues retrieving after the model has already converged on an answer, incurring calls that change neither the prediction nor the supporting evidence. The standard remedy is to learn a stopping policy from labeled trajectories. Adaptive-RAG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jeong et al., 2024</a>)</cite> trains a classifier to route questions to no-retrieval, single-step, or multi-step strategies. Stop-RAG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2025</a>)</cite> learns a value head over retrieval trajectories, while Self-RAG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Asai et al., 2023</a>)</cite> trains the language model to emit retrieve and critique tokens. All three place the stopping decision in a learned component that is less transparent and typically requires retraining for new models or tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This paper studies the stopping decision in isolation. We present <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TASR</span> (Training-free Adaptive Stopping Rule), a one-line predicate that stops the loop when the model repeats its previous-round normalized answer <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">and</em> the calibrated probability of being correct exceeds <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.25</annotation></semantics></math>. TASR uses no learned classifier or value head, and the threshold is fixed across all twenty-four (model, retriever, corpus) configurations we evaluate. It serves as a drop-in alternative to fixed call budgets and learned controllers  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Trivedi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Press et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jeong et al., 2024</a>)</cite> transferring across models and datasets without retraining.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions can be summarized as follows: (1) a one-line predicate that beats fixed-<math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}3</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+3.42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3.42</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+3.42</annotation></semantics></math> macro F1 on a <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{\times}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{\times}2</annotation></semantics></math> distractor grid at matched cost; (2) external validation on nine open-domain BM25 cells (HotpotQA-fullwiki, NQ-Open, TriviaQA-Open, all three models) confirming that the pattern generalizes; (3) full auditability using two scalars per round; (4) a grounded analysis showing that Gemma’s <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\sim}2\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∼</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}2\times</annotation></semantics></math> overconfident raw logit margins are corrected by isotonic calibration, explaining why TASR’s largest gains appear on the weakest model; and (5) a signal-quality diagnostic showing that verbalized confidence collapses on RLHF-tuned models while the logit margin preserves <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="44\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>44</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">44\times</annotation></semantics></math> better class-conditional separation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Iterative retrieval.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">IRCoT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Trivedi et al., 2023</a>)</cite> interleaves chain-of-thought generation with retrieval, using each reasoning step to guide the next query; it improves multi-hop QA on HotpotQA, 2WikiMultiHopQA, MuSiQue, and IIRC, but issues a retrieval call per CoT sentence with no internal stopping criterion. Self-Ask <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Press et al., 2023</a>)</cite> decomposes a complex question into follow-up sub-questions, retrieving evidence for each. FLARE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> predicts the next sentence and triggers retrieval whenever it contains a low-confidence token, then regenerates; this is the closest prior signal to the calibrated logit margin we use, but FLARE applies it within a single generation pass and uses it to decide <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">when to retrieve</em>, not when to stop. None of these works isolates the stopping decision as a standalone component.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Adaptive routing and learned stopping.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Adaptive-RAG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jeong et al., 2024</a>)</cite> trains a smaller language model on automatically derived complexity labels to classify each incoming question as no-retrieval, single-step, or multi-step, and applies the resulting strategy uniformly to the whole question. Self-RAG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Asai et al., 2023</a>)</cite> trains the generator language model itself to emit retrieve and critique special tokens, using a critic model supervised by GPT-4 to label training data. Stop-RAG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2025</a>)</cite> trains a value head on full retrieval trajectories to decide when to halt. All three approaches tie the stopping decision to a trained component and require retraining for each new model or task. TASR replaces the trained component with one equality check and one calibrated threshold.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Self-consistency and answer stability.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Self-consistency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> samples multiple independent reasoning chains at a fixed evidence set and selects the modal answer; agreement <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">across samples</em> signals correctness. Our answer-stable component is the iteration-level analogue: agreement across consecutive rounds within a single trajectory.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Calibration and verbalized confidence.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior work shows that verbalized confidence is often poorly calibrated in RLHF-tuned language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. This limits its usefulness as a decision signal and motivates the use of model-internal uncertainty measures instead. In our setting, we therefore use the logit gap at the answer-commitment token and calibrate it against correctness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evidence dilution.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Adding retrieved context can reduce performance through distracting evidence or context dilution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levy et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lahmy and Yozevitch, 2025</a>)</cite>. For iterative retrieval systems, this implies that additional rounds are not uniformly beneficial.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Method</h2>

<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.13814v1/TASR_Graph.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/110;" width="452" height="110"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>The TASR loop. The retriever ranks candidate paragraphs against the question once; each round reveals the next-ranked paragraph and calls the LLM. The raw answer is normalized to <math id="S3.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{a}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{a}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and compared with the previous round’s <math id="S3.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{a}_{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{a}_{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The first answer-token logit margin <math id="S3.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is isotonically calibrated on the tune split (offline) and compared against <math id="S3.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.25</annotation></semantics></math>. TASR fires when both checks pass; otherwise the loop continues until the <math id="S3.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-round budget is exhausted. §<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. The TASR predicate: AS_m25 ‣ 3. Method ‣ TASR: Training-Free Adaptive Stopping for Iterative Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> provides a worked example.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Agent and action space</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent operates in a two-action setting. At each round <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> the policy chooses between <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">stopping and answering</em> or <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">adding the next ranked paragraph to the evidence set</em>. There is no query reformulation: BM25 (or the dense retriever) ranks candidate paragraphs against the question once, and each round reveals the next passage in that fixed ranked list. Round-<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> state is exactly the top-<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> retrieved paragraphs. One LLM call per round produces a structured response: a short answer preceded by the literal token “Answer:”, a self-assessed <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> confidence, and the per-token logprobs from which the margin signal is computed. The maximum number of rounds is <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R{=}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R{=}5</annotation></semantics></math>. If no stopping rule fires, the agent returns the round-<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> answer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We add one paragraph per round to maximize the resolution of the stopping signal. Coarser granularity is left to future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Cached signals</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each round produces four signals that are cached to disk and used by any stopping rule. We group them by the sufficiency criterion they are intended to capture.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Answer stability (trajectory).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">answer_stable</span> <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> if the round-<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> answer, normalized with the standard HotpotQA scoring procedure (lowercase, strip articles, strip punctuation, collapse whitespace), equals the round-<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r{-}1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r{-}1)</annotation></semantics></math> normalized answer. It is defined for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Calibrated logit margin (model convergence).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">calibrated_logit_margin</span> <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is the calibrated probability that the round-<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> answer is exactly-match correct. The raw signal is <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">answer_token_margin</span>, the top-1 minus top-2 logprob at the first non-whitespace token after the literal string “Answer:” in the generated response, which is the point at which the model commits to its output. Calibration uses per-round isotonic regression to map <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">answer_token_margin</span> to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\mathrm{EM}{=}1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>EM</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\mathrm{EM}{=}1)</annotation></semantics></math>, fit on the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math>-question tune split for each cell. This signal preserves the model’s uncertainty even when the verbalized <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> confidence collapses to a single value, a known pathology on RLHF-tuned models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Lexical overlap (information novelty).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">overlap_signal</span> <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is the IDF-weighted token Jaccard between the round-<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> paragraph and the union of paragraphs <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,\ldots,r{-}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,\ldots,r{-}1</annotation></semantics></math>. High overlap means the new evidence is mostly redundant.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Verbalized confidence (model convergence).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">calibrated_conf</span> <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is the parsed <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> self-reported confidence, isotonically calibrated against per-row exact match on the tune split.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All four signals are persisted to a single per-round parquet, so every stopping rule is evaluated offline against the cache with no fresh LLM calls. Only <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">answer_stable</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">calibrated_logit_margin</span> enter the final rule. The other two signals are inputs to the search of §<a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3. Why the rule’s simplicity is a finding, not a shortcut ‣ 5. Results ‣ TASR: Training-Free Adaptive Stopping for Iterative Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.13814v1/figures/nats_margin_reliability.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/137;" width="405" height="137" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> Logit margin distribution by correctness (smoke test, <math id="S3.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}100</annotation></semantics></math> rows). Correct answers (blue) have mean margin <math id="S3.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.00" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.00</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.00</annotation></semantics></math> nats
vs. <math id="S3.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.91" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.91</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.91</annotation></semantics></math> for incorrect (red), a <math id="S3.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.08" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.08</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.08</annotation></semantics></math>-nat separation. <span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right:</span> Reliability diagram: <math id="S3.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\mathrm{EM}{=}1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>EM</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\mathrm{EM}{=}1)</annotation></semantics></math> in equal-size margin
buckets. The margin is monotonically informative despite noise at <math id="S3.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}20</annotation></semantics></math> per bucket.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Per-round isotonic calibration</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The calibrated logit margin uses <em id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">per-round</em> isotonic regression rather than a single global calibrator. This design choice is load-bearing. The empirical probability of correctness given the same confidence value varies substantially across rounds: on the Qwen tune split, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\mathrm{EM}{=}1\mid\mathrm{conf}{=}5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>EM</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>conf</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\mathrm{EM}{=}1\mid\mathrm{conf}{=}5)</annotation></semantics></math> rises from <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.442" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.442</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.442</annotation></semantics></math> at round <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.643" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.643</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.643</annotation></semantics></math> at round <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>, a <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math>-percentage-point swing reflecting the growing evidence set. A global calibrator averages away this round-dependent meaning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We fit one isotonic regression per round per cell using the pool-adjacent-violators (PAV) algorithm on the <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math>-question tune split (each round contributes <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> rows). The calibrator maps <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">answer_token_margin</span> <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to P(\mathrm{EM}{=}1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>EM</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to P(\mathrm{EM}{=}1)</annotation></semantics></math> monotonically. When the input distribution degenerates, for example, in round <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math> of verbalized confidence values are <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>), the calibrator collapses to the round-mean accuracy—a graceful fallback that still preserves round-level meaning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">On a separate ablation across <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18</annotation></semantics></math> (model, dataset, prompt) cells, per-round isotonic achieves a worse Brier score than global isotonic (<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.195" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.195</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.195</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.192" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.192</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.192</annotation></semantics></math>) but better rule-level F1 (<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="59.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>59.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">59.0</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="57.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>57.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">57.9</annotation></semantics></math>). The reversal illustrates that standard calibration metrics (Brier) are not the right target for threshold-based stopping rules: a calibrator that minimizes average prediction error can blur the decision boundary at the threshold that matters for the rule (see §<a href="#S6.SS3" title="6.3. Standard calibration metrics are the wrong target ‣ 6. Discussion ‣ TASR: Training-Free Adaptive Stopping for Iterative Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> for a fuller treatment of this phenomenon).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Platt scaling wins on <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18</annotation></semantics></math> cells (<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.41" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.41</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.41</annotation></semantics></math> F1 avg) but is less robust when the margin distribution is narrow or multi-modal; we retain isotonic for robustness on small <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>The TASR predicate: AS_m25</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">TASR is the rule <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AS_m25</span>, defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{stop}_{r}\;=\;\mathbf{1}\!\left[\,\tilde{a}_{r}=\tilde{a}_{r-1}\;\wedge\;m_{r}&gt;0.25\,\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>stop</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.502em">∧</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo lspace="0.170em">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{stop}_{r}\;=\;\mathbf{1}\!\left[\,\tilde{a}_{r}=\tilde{a}_{r-1}\;\wedge\;m_{r}&gt;0.25\,\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{a}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{a}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is the round-<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> normalized answer and <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is the calibrated logit margin. In other words: stop at the first round at which the model repeats its previous-round answer and the calibrated probability of being correct exceeds <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25\%</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction the rule cannot fire at <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{=}1</annotation></semantics></math>, so the minimum cost is two LLM calls per question.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The threshold <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.25</annotation></semantics></math> is selected by a Pareto-knee analysis: sweeping the margin floor from <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.20</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.35" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.35</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.35</annotation></semantics></math> in steps of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math>, moving from <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.20\to 0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.20</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.20\to 0.25</annotation></semantics></math> increases F1 by <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9.4</annotation></semantics></math> points per additional macro call while <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.25\to 0.30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.25</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0.30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.25\to 0.30</annotation></semantics></math> drops to <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.9</annotation></semantics></math>, marking a clear knee. The sweep is bracketed (pre-registered), single-knob, and run on the distractor setting. The threshold is locked across all twenty-four configurations and not tuned per cell.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The margin floor guards against wrong-and-stable answers: without it, the pure answer-stable rule fires on roughly <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\%</annotation></semantics></math> of trajectories where the model repeats an incorrect answer before the gold paragraph enters, and the <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.25</annotation></semantics></math> threshold converts roughly half of those premature stops into correct late-round answers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By construction the rule cannot fire at <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{=}1</annotation></semantics></math> (no prior answer to compare); a round-aware variant allowing an early high-confidence stop saves <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.7\%</annotation></semantics></math> of calls at an undetectable <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.17" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.17</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.17</annotation></semantics></math> F1 cost, which is not a meaningful Pareto extension.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Walk-through example.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3. Method ‣ TASR: Training-Free Adaptive Stopping for Iterative Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the loop structure. This paragraph traces one
real trajectory through it. Consider HotpotQA dev question <span id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">5a77e70f</span>: <em id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">“What is the title of
the 1979 film adaptation of William Shakespeare’s play in which the English
poet who wrote ‘Whale Nation’ played a main character?”</em> (gold answer:
<em id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">The Tempest</em>). BM25 ranks the ten paragraphs once; the top two are
“Heathcote Williams” (score <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32.5</annotation></semantics></math>) and “The Tempest (1979 film)”
(score <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>19.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19.8</annotation></semantics></math>). At <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{=}1</annotation></semantics></math> only the first paragraph is visible; the LLM
answers <em id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">“Titus Andronicus”</em> with calibrated margin <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1}{=}0.30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1}{=}0.30</annotation></semantics></math>.
A margin-only stopping rule (<math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{r}&gt;0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{r}&gt;0.25</annotation></semantics></math>) would halt here on a wrong
answer. AS_m25 does not fire because answer-stable is undefined at <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{=}1</annotation></semantics></math>.
At <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{=}2</annotation></semantics></math> the gold paragraph “The Tempest (1979 film)” enters the evidence;
the LLM revises to <em id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">“The Tempest”</em> (<math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{2}{=}0.81" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.81</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{2}{=}0.81</annotation></semantics></math>), but
<math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{a}_{2}\neq\tilde{a}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{a}_{2}\neq\tilde{a}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> so AS_m25 still does not fire.
At <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{=}3</annotation></semantics></math> the model repeats <em id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">“The Tempest”</em> (<math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{3}{=}0.80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.80</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{3}{=}0.80</annotation></semantics></math>):
both predicates are satisfied and the loop stops, returning the correct
answer in three calls rather than the fixed-<math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}5</annotation></semantics></math> budget.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distractor setting (primary).</span> <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HotpotQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2018</a>)</cite> distractor validation, sampled to <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="400" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>400</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">400</annotation></semantics></math> questions with seed <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>42</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42</annotation></semantics></math> and split <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> tune / <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>300</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">300</annotation></semantics></math> eval. <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2WikiMultiHopQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>)</cite> distractor validation, same sampling protocol. Both use the standard 10-paragraph pool (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> gold, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> distractor) per question.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Open-domain setting (external validity).</span> To test generalization beyond the curated distractor pool, we re-run the same pipeline on three open-domain BM25 corpora via prebuilt Pyserini Lucene indexes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2021</a>)</cite>: <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HotpotQA-fullwiki</span> (same <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>300</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">300</annotation></semantics></math> eval questions, retrieved from the full Wikipedia index), <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NQ-Open</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwiatkowski et al., 2019</a>)</cite> (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>300</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">300</annotation></semantics></math> questions subsampled with seed <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>42</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42</annotation></semantics></math> from DPR’s dev set), and <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TriviaQA-Open</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joshi et al., 2017</a>)</cite> (same protocol). This yields <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> additional cells: all three models on fullwiki, NQ, and TriviaQA.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Three open-weight instruction-tuned LLMs served via vLLM-compatible endpoints at temperature <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> with the top-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> logprobs returned per token: <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen 3.6-27B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen Team, 2026</a>)</cite>, <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Devstral-Small-2-24B-Instruct-2512</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mistral AI, 2025</a>)</cite>, and <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemma-4-31B-IT</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gemma Team, Google DeepMind, 2025</a>)</cite>. The distractor grid contains six cells; the open-domain extension adds nine more (§<a href="#S5.SS6" title="5.6. External validity: open-domain BM25 corpora ‣ 5. Results ‣ TASR: Training-Free Adaptive Stopping for Iterative Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>). Endpoint precision is bf16 weights with fp16 KV cache (vLLM defaults reported by the endpoint operators); we did not control or introspect the serving configuration.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines and caching.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Closed-book, fixed-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,2,3,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,2,3,5\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a per-question oracle (earliest correct round). Fixed-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}3</annotation></semantics></math> is the primary budget-matched baseline. All LLM responses are disk-cached, and every rule sweep is offline simulation on the cached parquet.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Statistics.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Paired bootstrap with <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> resamples and seed <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>42</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42</annotation></semantics></math>, paired by question id within a cell <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du, 2025</a>)</cite>. Confidence intervals are the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="97.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>97.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">97.5</annotation></semantics></math> percentiles of the resampled distribution.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Results</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Headline</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1. Headline ‣ 5. Results ‣ TASR: Training-Free Adaptive Stopping for Iterative Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reports macro F1 and macro calls across the six distractor cells. TASR reaches <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="57.40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>57.40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">57.40</annotation></semantics></math> F1 at <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.13</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.13</annotation></semantics></math> calls, exceeding fixed-<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}3</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+3.42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3.42</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+3.42</annotation></semantics></math> F1 at <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.13</annotation></semantics></math> calls and retaining <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="94.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>94.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">94.8\%</annotation></semantics></math> of fixed-<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}5</annotation></semantics></math>’s F1 at <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="62.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>62.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">62.6\%</annotation></semantics></math> of its calls. The per-question oracle reaches <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="65.26" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>65.26</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">65.26</annotation></semantics></math> F1 at <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.61" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.61</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.61</annotation></semantics></math> calls; it is below fixed-<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}3</annotation></semantics></math> on calls because it can stop at round <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> on questions the closed-book pass already answers correctly.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>Macro results across the <math id="S5.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> grid (<math id="S5.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}6</annotation></semantics></math> cells, <math id="S5.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>300</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">300</annotation></semantics></math> eval questions per cell).</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
