</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>Why the rule’s simplicity is a finding, not a shortcut</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">AS_m25 is the product of an exhaustive combinatorial search, not an unsearched prior. On the canonical HotpotQA-distractor setting (Qwen), we enumerated every non-empty OR-combination of eight per-round signals (the four of §<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Cached signals ‣ 3. Method ‣ TASR: Training-Free Adaptive Stopping for Iterative Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> plus four computed solely for this search): <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">answer_stable</span>, calibrated verbalized confidence, IDF-weighted lexical overlap, the BM25 top-score <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-score, the rank-<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> gap, an answer-in-evidence indicator, a cross-encoder reranker score (BGE-reranker-v2-m3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>), and an LLM-judge entailment score. This yielded <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{8}\!-\!1\,{=}\,255" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.448em">=</mo><mn> 255</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{8}\!-\!1\,{=}\,255</annotation></semantics></math> candidate rules; a second pass on a confidence-first prompt with six of these signals added <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{6}\!-\!1\,{=}\,63" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.448em">=</mo><mn> 63</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{6}\!-\!1\,{=}\,63</annotation></semantics></math> more (<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="318" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>318</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">318</annotation></semantics></math> OR-subsets). Of these, only two produced a <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95\%</annotation></semantics></math> CI excluding zero against fixed-<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}3</annotation></semantics></math>, and both were single-signal rules: answer-stability under the canonical prompt, and calibrated verbalized confidence under the confidence-first prompt. We then added the calibrated logit margin (§<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. The TASR predicate: AS_m25 ‣ 3. Method ‣ TASR: Training-Free Adaptive Stopping for Iterative Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>) and re-ran the enumeration over six signals (<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="63" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>63</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">63</annotation></semantics></math> more), from which AS_m25 emerged as the lone significant winner, for <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="381" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>381</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">381</annotation></semantics></math> enumerated rules in total. The same locked threshold is then applied unchanged to all twenty-four (model, retriever, corpus) configurations with no per-cell tuning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across all threshold sweeps, reranker variants, and several hundred further evaluations, no rule Pareto-dominates AS_m25. Two structural patterns explain why. First, OR-combining signals accelerates stopping in the wrong direction: each individually-tuned signal fires on roughly <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\%</annotation></semantics></math> of rounds, so an OR of two fires on <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}75\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}75\%</annotation></semantics></math>, triggering stops before evidence has accumulated. Second, AND-combining collapses to the strongest single constraint, which is AS_m25 itself. The rule’s leverage lies in its <em id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">form</em> (a two-term AND-gate) rather than in the size of the signal set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Five-fold cross-validation within each cell’s <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math>-question tune split further confirms that per-cell threshold tuning is unreliable at this sample size: train/eval F1 gaps of <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> points appear on four of six cells. A direct comparison shows the single locked threshold (<math id="S5.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.25</annotation></semantics></math>) is <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.18</annotation></semantics></math> macro F1 <em id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">better</em> than per-cell tuning, making the lock a principled choice rather than a simplification.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4. </span>Negative results on alternative signals</h3>

<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cross-encoder reranker.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Adding a cross-encoder reranker (BGE-reranker-v2-m3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>) as a fifth OR-vote drops macro F1 by <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>14</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14</annotation></semantics></math> points across cells (all CIs exclude zero). The reranker fires at round <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> on nearly every question because it scores evidence topicality rather than support for the model’s current answer, short-circuiting the rule on still-wrong rounds. A per-question <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-score variant is a flat null.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Continuous text stability.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Relaxing the binary check to a continuous token-F1 or SequenceMatcher threshold gains at most <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.10</annotation></semantics></math> F1 (CI <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.00,+0.38]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.00</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.38</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.00,+0.38]</annotation></semantics></math>) across all thresholds and cells. At temperature <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> with HotpotQA normalization, the model produces highly stable text and there are no near-miss answers to recover.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">BM25 retrieval confidence.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A robust <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-score of the top BM25 score (median/IQR normalization) achieves <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.568" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.568</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.568</annotation></semantics></math> nats of class-conditional separation between correct and incorrect rows, a substantial improvement over the original global <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-score (<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.089" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.089</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.089</annotation></semantics></math> nats, which is reverse-correlated). As a stopping rule, however, adding it to AS_m25 as an OR-clause <em id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hurts</em> F1 by <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.5</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>17.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17.9</annotation></semantics></math> points across all thresholds tested. The retrieval-confidence signal fires too eagerly: it triggers stops on questions where BM25 is unambiguous but the LLM still gets the answer wrong. LLM-side signals dominate retriever-side signals for stopping.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Distribution margin aggregations.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On a 50-question pilot, replacing the first-answer-token margin with distribution-level aggregations (min, 10th-percentile, mean, median across all answer tokens) improves feature-level Pearson correlation with EM by up to <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+57\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>57</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+57\%</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.60</annotation></semantics></math> for <span id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">margin_p10</span> vs. <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.38" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.38</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.38</annotation></semantics></math> for <span id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">margin_first</span>). At the rule level, however, substituting any aggregation into AS_m25 changes macro F1 by at most <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 0.22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>0.22</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 0.22</annotation></semantics></math>—within noise. The AND-gate with answer-stability is the binding constraint; margin improvements are wasted when the gate is closed.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5. </span>Signal robustness: tune vs. evaluation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.5. Signal robustness: tune vs. evaluation ‣ 5. Results ‣ TASR: Training-Free Adaptive Stopping for Iterative Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> reports per-signal class-conditional Cohen’s <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> (correct vs. incorrect rows on the EM metric) on both the tuning split (<math id="S5.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> questions) and the held-out evaluation split (<math id="S5.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>300</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">300</annotation></semantics></math> questions), computed across all three models on the HotpotQA distractor cell, with Qwen shown as the representative case. The headline signal is the only competitive signal whose separation <em id="S5.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strengthens</em> from tune to evaluation. The two-pass entailment judge, which has the highest tune-set <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> of any signal we tested, loses more than half its separation on the held-out split.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3. </span>Class-conditional Cohen’s <math id="S5.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> (correct vs. incorrect on EM) by split. Positive <math id="S5.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> means the signal strengthens on evaluation. Qwen shown as representative; Devstral and Gemma show the same ordering.</figcaption>
