<td id="S5.T5.2.13.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><math id="S5.T5.m64" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{68.67}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="bold">68.67</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{68.67}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.T5.2.13.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> / <math id="S5.T5.m65" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.34" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.34</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.34</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.13.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><math id="S5.T5.m66" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{+2.59}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">+</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="bold">2.59</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{+2.59}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.13.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T5.2.13.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">[+0.17, +5.07]</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Pareto pattern survives both swaps. Across all nine cells, no cell regresses significantly; two are significant wins (both Gemma: NQ-Open <math id="S5.SS7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+3.26" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3.26</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+3.26</annotation></semantics></math>, TriviaQA-Open <math id="S5.SS7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+2.59" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2.59</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+2.59</annotation></semantics></math>), and seven are ties. Both wins are on the model that gains most on BM25 (§<a href="#S5.SS8" title="5.8. Cross-model analysis: why Gemma benefits most ‣ 5. Results ‣ TASR: Training-Free Adaptive Stopping for Iterative Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>): the retriever changes while the model-side failure mode the rule corrects does not. Calibration transfer is the key test: the
isotonic was fit on BM25 logit margins from one corpus and applied unchanged to dense evidence from two retriever families on three corpora,
TASR calls stay in <math id="S5.SS7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.5</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.8</annotation></semantics></math> across all nine cells, matching the BM25 range (<math id="S5.SS7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.5</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS7.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.1</annotation></semantics></math>), so the calibrator did not silently fail.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.8. </span>Cross-model analysis: why Gemma benefits most</h3>

<div id="S5.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Gemma is the weakest model on every cell yet receives the largest TASR lift. Table <a href="#S5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 5.8. Cross-model analysis: why Gemma benefits most ‣ 5. Results ‣ TASR: Training-Free Adaptive Stopping for Iterative Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> reports four converging lines of evidence for a grounded hypothesis: Gemma combines a weak single-pass baseline with overconfident raw logit margins, and the per-round isotonic calibration is what corrects this.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6. </span>Cross-model evidence for Gemma’s overconfidence pattern. Raw and calibrated margins are means across each model’s open-domain cells. <math id="S5.T6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>F1 is TASR minus fixed-<math id="S5.T6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}3</annotation></semantics></math>, macro across respective cells.</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T6.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T6.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"></td>
<th id="S5.T6.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T6.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Qwen</span></th>
<th id="S5.T6.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T6.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Devstral</span></th>
<th id="S5.T6.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T6.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Gemma</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S5.T6.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T6.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T6.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">fixed-<math id="S5.T6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}1</annotation></semantics></math> F1 (open-domain macro)</span></td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="41.71" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">41.71</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">41.71</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="43.47" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">43.47</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">43.47</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30.27" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">30.27</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30.27</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T6.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T6.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T6.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">raw margin (nats, open-domain mean)</span></td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.66" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">4.66</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.66</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.62" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">3.62</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.62</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9.13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">9.13</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9.13</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T6.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T6.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T6.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">calibrated margin (open-domain mean)</span></td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.52" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.52</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.52</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.49</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.49</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.40</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T6.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T6.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.T6.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">F1 vs. <math id="S5.T6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}3</annotation></semantics></math> (distractor macro)</span></td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1.46" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1.46</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1.46</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.23</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.23</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+8.59" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">8.59</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+8.59</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T6.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T6.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.T6.2.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">F1 vs. <math id="S5.T6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}3</annotation></semantics></math> (open-domain macro)</span></td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.47" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.47</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.47</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T6.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.60</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T6.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+2.57" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2.57</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+2.57</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Gemma’s single-pass baseline is <math id="S5.SS8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>13</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13</annotation></semantics></math> F1 points below Qwen and Devstral, yet its raw logit margins are <math id="S5.SS8.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\sim}2\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∼</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}2\times</annotation></semantics></math> larger, indicating overconfidence. Isotonic calibration reverses the ordering (Gemma lowest at <math id="S5.SS8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.40</annotation></semantics></math>), mapping each model’s margins onto a common <math id="S5.SS8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\mathrm{EM})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>EM</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\mathrm{EM})</annotation></semantics></math> scale so one locked threshold works across models. Because TASR spends more rounds on Gemma’s less-stable trajectories (avg <math id="S5.SS8.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.62" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.62</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.62</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS8.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.78" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.78</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.78</annotation></semantics></math> calls vs. <math id="S5.SS8.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.53" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.53</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.53</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS8.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.85" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.85</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.85</annotation></semantics></math> for Qwen), the extra rounds buy real F1: <math id="S5.SS8.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+8.59" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>8.59</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+8.59</annotation></semantics></math> on distractor, <math id="S5.SS8.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+2.57" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2.57</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+2.57</annotation></semantics></math> on open-domain, against <math id="S5.SS8.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1.46" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1.46</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1.46</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S5.SS8.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.47" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.47</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.47</annotation></semantics></math> for Qwen.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS9" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.9. </span>Cross-prompt robustness</h3>

<div id="S5.SS9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We re-ran the rule sweep on a <em id="S5.SS9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">confidence-first</em> prompt variant in which the model emits its <math id="S5.SS9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> confidence before committing to an answer. The reorder spreads the confidence distribution (entropy increases from <math id="S5.SS9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.182" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.182</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.182</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.839" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.839</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.839</annotation></semantics></math> nats; the canonical prompt emits “confidence <math id="S5.SS9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=5</annotation></semantics></math>” on <math id="S5.SS9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="96.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>96.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">96.5\%</annotation></semantics></math> of rounds) at the cost of <math id="S5.SS9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> F1 in raw accuracy (round-5 F1 drops from <math id="S5.SS9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="69.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>69.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">69.4</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="63.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>63.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">63.8</annotation></semantics></math>): the model is more willing to abstain when confidence precedes commitment. Under the confidence-first prompt, the headline shifts: the rule <span id="S5.SS9.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">calibrated_conf</span> <math id="S5.SS9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;0.552" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.552</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;0.552</annotation></semantics></math> wins at <math id="S5.SS9.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+5.84" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>5.84</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+5.84</annotation></semantics></math> F1 <math id="S5.SS9.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[+2.96,+8.86]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2.96</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>8.86</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[+2.96,+8.86]</annotation></semantics></math> vs. fixed-<math id="S5.SS9.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}3</annotation></semantics></math>, while <span id="S5.SS9.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">answer_stable</span> drops to a tie (<math id="S5.SS9.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.26" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.26</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.26</annotation></semantics></math> F1 <math id="S5.SS9.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-2.60,+1.91]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2.60</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1.91</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-2.60,+1.91]</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Two consequences. (i) The <em id="S5.SS9.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">form</em> of the headline rule generalizes to prompt design: the single best signal in the model-convergence family wins on both prompts, but <em id="S5.SS9.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which</em> convergence signal is best depends on prompt structure. (ii) OR-combining signals never beats the best singleton in either prompt regime: across <math id="S5.SS9.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="381" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>381</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">381</annotation></semantics></math> OR-subset rules covering both prompts, only the two singletons clear the CI bar. Dropping the confidence request entirely leaves the rule unaffected (<math id="S5.SS9.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math> F1), since TASR uses only the logit margin and answer text.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.13814v1/figures/nats_confidence_collapse.png" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/131;" width="405" height="131" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5. </span>Verbalized confidence distributions under two prompt variants and the uniform reference. The canonical prompt collapses to <math id="S5.F5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="96.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>96.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">96.5\%</annotation></semantics></math> mass
on confidence <math id="S5.F5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=5</annotation></semantics></math> (entropy <math id="S5.F5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.182" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.182</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.182</annotation></semantics></math> nats); the confidence-first variant spreads to <math id="S5.F5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="62.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>62.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">62.7\%</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S5.F5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.839" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.839</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.839</annotation></semantics></math> nats) but remains far below the <math id="S5.F5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.609" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.609</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.609</annotation></semantics></math>-nat
maximum.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS10" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.10. </span>Question-type breakdown</h3>

<div id="S5.SS10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">HotpotQA labels each question as <em id="S5.SS10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bridge</em> (multi-hop chained facts) or <em id="S5.SS10.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">comparison</em> (parallel facts about two entities). On the Qwen eval split (<math id="S5.SS10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="241" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>241</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">241</annotation></semantics></math> bridge, <math id="S5.SS10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="59" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>59</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">59</annotation></semantics></math> comparison), the answer-stability signal adapts to difficulty implicitly: on bridge questions it averages <math id="S5.SS10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.59" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.59</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.59</annotation></semantics></math> calls and gains <math id="S5.SS10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+2.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+2.05</annotation></semantics></math> F1 over fixed-<math id="S5.SS10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}3</annotation></semantics></math>; on comparison questions it stops earlier (<math id="S5.SS10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.12</annotation></semantics></math> calls) and ties on F1. The agent never receives the question-type label. This implicit adaptation is a consequence of the trajectory structure: comparison questions tend to stabilize earlier because the model can often resolve the comparison from the first two paragraphs, while bridge questions require chaining facts across paragraphs and take longer to converge.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Discussion</h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1. </span>Why a one-line rule works</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Answer stability is a <em id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">trajectory</em> signal: it tests whether the model has stopped changing its mind, not whether retrieval has converged or whether confidence is high. The margin floor is a <em id="S6.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">snapshot</em> guard: it withholds stopping when the model echoes a low-confidence answer. Every stopping decision reconstructs from two scalars per round, making it fully auditable without a separate probing setup. This also explains why combining signals via OR hurts: each individually-tuned snapshot signal fires on <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math><math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\%</annotation></semantics></math> of rows, so an OR of two fires on <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math><math id="S6.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="75\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">75\%</annotation></semantics></math>, pulling stops too early.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2. </span>Confidence collapse and why margin is necessary</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under the canonical prompt, <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="96.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>96.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">96.5\%</annotation></semantics></math> of verbalized confidence values are <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding an entropy of <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.182" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.182</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.182</annotation></semantics></math> nats (Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.9. Cross-prompt robustness ‣ 5. Results ‣ TASR: Training-Free Adaptive Stopping for Iterative Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). This is consistent across all three models, suggesting the collapse is a consequence of instruction-tuning and RLHF rather than a model-specific artifact. When isotonic calibration maps this degenerate distribution, it produces a single bucket: <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{conf}{=}5\to\sim 0.57" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>conf</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>0.57</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{conf}{=}5\to\sim 0.57</annotation></semantics></math> calibrated probability. The result is one effective category—useless for threshold rules.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The logit margin avoids this pathology because it is continuous (range <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 0.3</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12.8</annotation></semantics></math> nats on the Qwen tune split), so every training row contributes real variation to the PAV algorithm. Class-conditional separation is <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.08" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.08</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.08</annotation></semantics></math> nats for the margin vs. <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.07" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.07</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.07</annotation></semantics></math> nats for self-reported confidence, a <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="44\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>44</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">44\times</annotation></semantics></math> difference. On the Brier score, the margin achieves <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.199" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.199</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.199</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.235" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.235</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.235</annotation></semantics></math> for confidence and <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.246" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.246</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.246</annotation></semantics></math> for the uninformative baseline: margin is <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="4\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times</annotation></semantics></math> better than confidence at beating the baseline (<math id="S6.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.048" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.048</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.048</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.011" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.011</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.011</annotation></semantics></math> Brier delta).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A confidence-first prompt variant—in which the model emits its <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S6.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> score before committing to an answer—spreads the distribution (entropy <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.839" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.839</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.839</annotation></semantics></math> nats, <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="62.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>62.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">62.7\%</annotation></semantics></math> mass on <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>) at the cost of <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S6.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> F1 in raw accuracy. Under this prompt the headline rule shifts from answer-stability to calibrated confidence, but the structural pattern is preserved: the best single signal wins, and no combination beats it. The rule’s form (AND-gate over the best convergence signal) generalizes across prompt designs; <em id="S6.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which</em> convergence signal wins depends on how much information the prompt format preserves.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3. </span>Standard calibration metrics are the wrong target</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A recurring pattern in our ablations is that the calibrator with the lower Brier score does <em id="S6.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> produce the better stopping rule. Global isotonic has Brier <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.192" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.192</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.192</annotation></semantics></math> vs. per-round’s <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.195" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.195</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.195</annotation></semantics></math>, yet per-round wins on rule F1 by <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1.1</annotation></semantics></math>. Platt scaling has Brier <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.197" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.197</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.197</annotation></semantics></math> vs. isotonic’s <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.195" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.195</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.195</annotation></semantics></math>, yet wins on rule F1 by <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.41" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.41</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.41</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12/18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12/18</annotation></semantics></math> cells. The explanation is structural: Brier measures average prediction accuracy across the entire input space, but rule F1 depends on prediction accuracy specifically at the decision boundary. A calibrator can have low average error (good Brier) but blur the threshold region (bad rule F1), or higher average error (worse Brier) but a sharper threshold region (better rule F1). For practitioners building threshold-based stopping rules, we recommend evaluating calibrators by downstream rule metrics, not by standard calibration scores.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math>-question tune split is small; per-round isotonic calibration fit on as few as <math id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> rows, and degenerate rounds (e.g., round <math id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math> of values collapse to a single margin value) fall back to the round-mean accuracy rather than a learned mapping. The evaluation covers multi-hop QA only; whether the answer-stability signal generalizes to other RAG tasks (fact verification, long-form generation, conversational search) is untested. The rule trades a fatter call-distribution tail (P95 of <math id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> for fixed-<math id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}3</annotation></semantics></math>) for a lower mean, which makes it unsuitable for latency-bound deployments where worst-case cost matters more than amortized cost. Devstral shows small regressions on open-domain (<math id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.4</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.8</annotation></semantics></math> F1), indicating the rule does not help models with strong single-pass baselines. The Gemma overconfidence analysis (§<a href="#S5.SS8" title="5.8. Cross-model analysis: why Gemma benefits most ‣ 5. Results ‣ TASR: Training-Free Adaptive Stopping for Iterative Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>) is observational; we show correlation between raw margin magnitude and accuracy loss, but do not identify a causal mechanism.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">TASR is an auditable, training-free Pareto baseline against which learned stopping controllers can be compared. It demonstrates that a two-term conjunction with one locked threshold suffices to Pareto-dominate fixed call budgets across a broad range of evaluation conditions. On the primary <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-model <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dataset distractor grid, the rule exceeds fixed-<math id="S7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}3</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+3.42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3.42</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+3.42</annotation></semantics></math> macro F1 while retaining <math id="S7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="94.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>94.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">94.8\%</annotation></semantics></math> of fixed-<math id="S7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}5</annotation></semantics></math>’s F1 at <math id="S7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="62.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>62.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">62.6\%</annotation></semantics></math> of its calls. The pattern transfers without modification to nine open-domain BM25 cells and, with calibration locked from the distractor split, to nine dense-retrieval cells across two retriever families, producing zero significant regressions. The consistency across retrieval paradigms indicates that the stopping signal is a property of the model rather than the retrieval system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The calibration analysis explains why a single threshold transfers across three architecturally distinct models. Per-round isotonic regression maps model-specific logit margins onto a common <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\mathrm{EM})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>EM</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\mathrm{EM})</annotation></semantics></math> scale, making one threshold meaningful across all three models. This design addresses a measurable pathology: verbalized <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> confidence collapses on RLHF-tuned models. Exhaustive evaluation of <math id="S7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="381" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>381</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">381</annotation></semantics></math> candidate stopping rules found no alternative that Pareto-dominates TASR, and additional signals consistently reduced performance.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
